The compiler rewrites an iterator method into a **state machine class** implementing {{IEnumerator<T>}}. Each {{yield return}} saves local state and a state number; {{MoveNext}} resumes after the last yield. Execution is lazy and streaming: only one element is produced at a time, so you can model infinite sequences and process huge inputs in constant memory.

Gotchas: argument validation inside an iterator method runs on first {{MoveNext}}, not at call time (split into a public validating method and a private iterator); {{yield}} cannot be inside {{try/catch}} (it can in {{try/finally}}); the iterator runs on the consumer's thread.

~~~csharp
public static IEnumerable<T> Where<T>(IEnumerable<T> src, Func<T, bool> p)
{
    foreach (var x in src) if (p(x)) yield return x;
}
~~~

An {{async}} method becomes a **state machine** (a struct in release builds, to avoid allocation on the synchronous path). At each {{await}}, if the awaited operation has not completed, the method registers a continuation and **returns** its Task to the caller, freeing the thread. When the operation completes, the continuation resumes the state machine, on the captured {{SynchronizationContext}}/{{TaskScheduler}} unless {{ConfigureAwait(false)}} is used.

A {{Task}} represents a future result; it is a **promise**, not necessarily a thread. For I/O there is no thread blocked at all (the OS completion port signals completion). For CPU work, {{Task.Run}} queues to the thread pool.

